a. that to guarantee Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in accordance with their dignity and to guarantee their right to life in accordance with their nature and disposition without discrimination and to receive Protection from Violence and other violations, legal certainty is necessary;

b. that to provide legal certainty in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection as well as to carry out the mandatory affairs of the Regional government, regulation in the form of a Regional regulation is necessary;

c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of :

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic Number 3886);

3. Law Number 6 of 2001 concerning the Establishment of Prabumulih City (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2001 Number 86, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4113);

4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);

5. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);

6. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);

7. Law Number 11 of 2020 concerning Job Creation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2020 Number 245, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6573);

8. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 56);

9. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 11 of 2011 concerning Policy for the Development of Child-Friendly Regencies/Cities (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 168);

10. Regulation of the Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 4 of 2018 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment of Regional Technical Implementation Units for Women's and Children's Protection (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2018 Number 532).

With Mutual Approval of THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PRABUMULIH CITY and THE MAYOR OF PRABUMULIH DECIDES:

To Establish : REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION.

Article 17

In order to prevent acts of Violence against women and children, the City Government:

a. conducts socialization regarding the prevention of Violence against women and children by optimizing the role of customary institutions, Education, culture, religion and community organizations;

b. builds networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children victims of Violence;

c. provides public facilities that are comfortable and safe for women and children; and

d. establishes an Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment P2TP2A established by decree of the Mayor and provides a Secretariat for the Integrated Service Center for Women and Children Empowerment.

Article 58

(1) Facilitation of the juvenile justice system includes criminal and civil justice.

(2) Facilitation of the criminal justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:

a. provision of companions or Child volunteers during examination by investigators;

b. provision of legal and psychological assistance services;

c. involving members/investigators of the local police in efforts to prevent criminal acts committed by Children or Children as victims;

d. realizing a restorative justice process for Children in conflict with the law; and

e. rehabilitation and social reintegration services.

(3) Fulfillment of Children's rights in the civil justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) includes the provision and delivery of consultation and legal aid services during the judicial process.

(4) In facilitating the juvenile justice system as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), the authorized Regional Government is the Regional Government in charge of empowerment and Protection of women and Children and has the obligation to accompany child victims and perpetrators in police, prosecution and court examinations.

Article 70

(1) The City Government is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of Women's empowerment and protection of Child victims of Violence.

(2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:

a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;

b. technical guidance and training;

c. provision of facilities;

d. monitoring; and

e. evaluation.

(3) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is in order to realize the objectives of empowerment and Protection of women and Child victims of Violence in accordance with minimum service standards.

Article 28

(1) Law enforcement and legal aid services are aimed at providing assistance and Protection to women and children victims of acts of Violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.

(2) Types of legal aid services include:

a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of examination reports;

b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;

c. legal aid in the examination process at court;

d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and

e. legal aid in the mediation process.

(3) The City Government provides legal aid to women and children victims of acts of Violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

(4) In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Article 4

The scope of regulation for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection includes:

a. protection of women;

b. protection of Children;

c. fulfillment of Children's rights;

d. improvement of quality of life for women and children;

e. community participation; and

f. guidance, supervision, assistance.

g. gender and child data system, disaggregated data on women and children;

Article 19

Forms of integrated services for women and children victims of acts of Violence include:

a. complaint handling;

b. health rehabilitation;

c. social rehabilitation;

d. victim assistance, enforcement and legal aid; and

e. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 67

(1) In order to prevent violations of women's rights, the community:

a. monitors various conditions occurring in their environment that may lead to violations of women's rights;

b. increases awareness of violations of women's rights;

c. strengthens the role of community organizations, religious organizations and the family environment; and

d. supervises the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection organized by the City Government with a mechanism for conveying aspirations to the Mayor or the Regional House of Representatives.

(2) In order to prevent violations of Children's rights, the community:

a. monitors various conditions occurring in their environment that lead to violations of Children's rights;

b. increases awareness of violations of Children's rights; and

c. strengthens the role of community organizations, religious organizations and the family environment; and prevents early marriage.

Article 5

(1) In order to improve the quality of life for women and children, the City Government is responsible for:

a. respecting and guaranteeing the rights of women and children without discrimination in accordance with human dignity;

b. enhancing Women's Empowerment and child protection in terms of role, quality and capacity in the fields of:

1. politics;

2. law;

3. social;

4. economy; and

5. culture.

c. strengthening and developing service provider institutions for Women's Empowerment and child protection in the City.

(2) The responsibility of the City Government in efforts to improve the quality of life for women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

1.8.text:
Article 16

(1) The City Government in its efforts to Protect women and children is responsible for:

a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children;

b. making efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women and children including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;

c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women and children victims of acts of Violence;

d. increasing Protection efforts for female workers;

e. strengthening and developing institutions providing Protection services for women and children;

f. cooperating with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, business world and/or NGOs in efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women and children; and

g. involving the active role of families, customary leaders, religious leaders, communities, and institutions in the field of Protection of women and children.

(2) The responsibility of the City Government in efforts to Protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions and may coordinate with relevant agencies.

Article 18

(1) Protection of women and children victims of acts of Violence is carried out through integrated services.

(2) The provision of integrated services for women and children victims of acts of Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) uses the principles of:

a. gender responsive;

b. non-discrimination;

c. equal and respectful relationships;

d. maintaining confidentiality;

e. providing a sense of security and comfort;

f. respecting individual differences;

g. non-judgmental;

h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;

i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;

j. fast and simple;

k. empathy; and

l. fulfillment of the rights of women and children.

Article 53

(1) The implementation of social welfare is directed to:

a. individuals;

b. families;

c. groups; and/or

d. communities.

(2) The implementation of social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be prioritized for those who have lives that are not humanely decent and have social problem criteria:

a. poverty;

b. abandonment;

c. disability;

d. remoteness;

e. social deviance and behavioral deviation;

f. disaster victims; and/or

g. victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination.

(3) Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services:

a. health;

b. education;

c. legal aid;

d. assistance;

e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f. social rehabilitation; and/or

g. reintegration.

Article 23

(1) Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 19 letter c is aimed at restoring and developing the capacity of women and children victims of acts of Violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.

(2) Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:

a. motivation and psychosocial diagnosis;

b. care and nurturing;

c. vocational training and entrepreneurship development;

d. spiritual mental guidance;

e. physical guidance;

f. social guidance and psychosocial counseling;

g. accessibility services;

h. social assistance and aid;

i. resocialization guidance;

j. follow-up guidance; and/or

k. referral.

(3) The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is organized by:

a. Regional Apparatus having duties and functions in the social field;

b. Regional Apparatus in charge of Women's and Children's Empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or social community organizations that already exist; or c. the ministry of religion.

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:

a. guarantee the fulfillment of women's empowerment and child protection in the City;

b. serve as a guideline in planning, policy and strategy for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

c. protect and prevent women and Children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of women and Children;

d. enhance the role and value of local wisdom as well as the role of customs in Women's Empowerment and Child Protection; and

e. serve as a legal umbrella for Regional Apparatus within the Prabumulih City Government that oversees women's empowerment and child protection in order to provide assistance and support to women and child victims starting from investigation at the police, prosecution to trial in court.

Article 22

(1) Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 19 letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of:

a. promotive;

b. preventive;

c. curative; and

d. rehabilitative.

(2) Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours, with quality and carried out in accordance with health service standards.

(3) Women and children victims of acts of Violence and children in conflict with the law have the right to free health services, including:

a. medical services;

b. medicolegal services;

c. psychosocial counseling; and

d. visum.

(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

