Article 28

(1) Law enforcement and legal aid services are aimed at providing assistance and Protection to women and children victims of acts of Violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.

(2) Types of legal aid services include:

a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of examination reports;

b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;

c. legal aid in the examination process at court;

d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and

e. legal aid in the mediation process.

(3) The City Government provides legal aid to women and children victims of acts of Violence based on the provisions of laws and regulations.

(4) In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regional Government may cooperate with legal aid institutions, women crisis centers, advocates and/or other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Article 24

(1) Counseling services as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:

a. assist women and children victims of acts of violence in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them themselves;

b. empower women and children victims of acts of Violence to determine their future;

c. provide moral support to women and children victims of acts of Violence in facing the process they are undergoing; and

d. make women and children victims of acts of Violence feel accepted in the community environment.

(2) Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:

a. counselors;

b. psychologists; and

c. psychiatrists.

(3) Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women and children victims of acts of Violence.

(4) The stages of counseling services include:

a. initial counseling;

b. follow-up counseling;

c. case discussion; and

d. site visits.

Article 22

(1) Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 19 letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of:

a. promotive;

b. preventive;

c. curative; and

d. rehabilitative.

(2) Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours, with quality and carried out in accordance with health service standards.

(3) Women and children victims of acts of Violence and children in conflict with the law have the right to free health services, including:

a. medical services;

b. medicolegal services;

c. psychosocial counseling; and

d. visum.

(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

