Article 23

(1) Social rehabilitation as referred to in Article 19 letter c is aimed at restoring and developing the capacity of women and children victims of acts of Violence who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.

(2) Social rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services:

a. motivation and psychosocial diagnosis;

b. care and nurturing;

c. vocational training and entrepreneurship development;

d. spiritual mental guidance;

e. physical guidance;

f. social guidance and psychosocial counseling;

g. accessibility services;

h. social assistance and aid;

i. resocialization guidance;

j. follow-up guidance; and/or

k. referral.

(3) The provision of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is organized by:

a. Regional Apparatus having duties and functions in the social field;

b. Regional Apparatus in charge of Women's and Children's Empowerment, and may cooperate with integrated service centers, women crisis centers or social community organizations that already exist; or c. the ministry of religion.

Article 24

(1) Counseling services as referred to in Article 23 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:

a. assist women and children victims of acts of violence in recognizing their problems and finding effective ways to overcome them themselves;

b. empower women and children victims of acts of Violence to determine their future;

c. provide moral support to women and children victims of acts of Violence in facing the process they are undergoing; and

d. make women and children victims of acts of Violence feel accepted in the community environment.

(2) Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:

a. counselors;

b. psychologists; and

c. psychiatrists.

(3) Counseling services are carried out in a place that guarantees a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from women and children victims of acts of Violence.

(4) The stages of counseling services include:

a. initial counseling;

b. follow-up counseling;

c. case discussion; and

d. site visits.

Article 16

(1) The City Government in its efforts to Protect women and children is responsible for:

a. establishing and implementing policies, programs and activities for the protection of women and children;

b. making efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women and children including criminal acts of trafficking in persons;

c. increasing the effectiveness of services for women and children victims of acts of Violence;

d. increasing Protection efforts for female workers;

e. strengthening and developing institutions providing Protection services for women and children;

f. cooperating with the Government, other Regional Governments, the private sector, business world and/or NGOs in efforts to prevent acts of Violence against women and children; and

g. involving the active role of families, customary leaders, religious leaders, communities, and institutions in the field of Protection of women and children.

(2) The responsibility of the City Government in efforts to Protect women as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions and may coordinate with relevant agencies.

Article 22

(1) Health rehabilitation as referred to in Article 19 letter b constitutes efforts that include aspects of:

a. promotive;

b. preventive;

c. curative; and

d. rehabilitative.

(2) Health rehabilitation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out comprehensively, accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours, with quality and carried out in accordance with health service standards.

(3) Women and children victims of acts of Violence and children in conflict with the law have the right to free health services, including:

a. medical services;

b. medicolegal services;

c. psychosocial counseling; and

d. visum.

(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (3) are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 53

(1) The implementation of social welfare is directed to:

a. individuals;

b. families;

c. groups; and/or

d. communities.

(2) The implementation of social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be prioritized for those who have lives that are not humanely decent and have social problem criteria:

a. poverty;

b. abandonment;

c. disability;

d. remoteness;

e. social deviance and behavioral deviation;

f. disaster victims; and/or

g. victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination.

(3) Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of provision of services:

a. health;

b. education;

c. legal aid;

d. assistance;

e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f. social rehabilitation; and/or

g. reintegration.

Article 64

The City Government, in order to provide protection to Children:

a. implements and supports national policies in the implementation of Child Protection in the City;

b. guarantees Children to exercise their rights in expressing opinions in accordance with the age and intelligence level of Children;

c. prevents Violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary institutions;

d. conducts socialization, seminars and counseling regarding Protection and Children's rights;

e. develops child-friendly school models;

f. provides public facilities that are friendly and safe for Children; and

g. encourages public awareness of the importance of Protection for Children.

